.TH lsmd.conf "5" "January 2015" "lsmd.conf @VERSION@" "libStorageMgmt daemon config"
.SH NAME
lsmd.conf - libStorageMgmt daemon lsmd configuration file.

.SH DESCRIPTION
The libStorageMgmt plugin daemon (\fBlsmd\fR) will read the \fBlsmd.conf\fR
file in the folder defined via \fB--confdir\fR (default is \fB/etc/lsm/\fR).

The daemon reads the plugin configuration files from the sub-folder
\fBpluginconf.d\fR. The plugin configuration file should be named as
<uri_scheme>.conf, for example:

    * MegaRAID plugin (\fBmegaraid\fR://):   \fBmegaraid.conf\fR

The \fBlsmd.conf\fR file controls the global settings for \fBlsmd\fR while
the plugin configuration file for each plugin controls individual plugin behavior.

Each option line of the configuration file should contain a trailing
semicolon(\fB;\fR).

.SH lsmd.conf OPTIONS
.TP
\fBallow-plugin-root-privilege = true;\fR

Indicates whether the \fBlsmd\fR daemon should keep running as root mode and invoke
plugin as root user when needed.

Without this option or with option set as \fBfalse\fR means that the daemon and
the plugins will never run as root.

Only when all the following requirements are met, will \fBlsmd\fR run specified
plugins as root user:

    1. "allow-plugin-root-privilege = true;" in lsmd.conf
    2. "require-root-privilege = true;" in plugin config
    3. API connection (or lsmcli) has root privileges

.TP
\fBmax-connections-per-uid = 64;\fR

Maximum number of plugin processes \fBlsmd\fR will run concurrently on behalf
of a single client user id.

\fBlsmd\fR forks a plugin process for every connection it accepts, so without
a limit a single local user can occupy the daemon's entire process budget.
Further connections from a user already at the limit are closed immediately,
and the client sees them as a failure to communicate with the daemon; existing
connections are unaffected.

Refused connections, whether by this limit or by the rate limit below, are
logged at most once every few seconds per user id, with a count of the
refusals that were not logged in between.

The default is \fB64\fR, far above what any normal client needs. Set it to
\fB0\fR for no limit. A negative value is ignored and the default is used.

.TP
\fBmax-connections-per-second-per-uid = 8;\fR

Maximum rate at which \fBlsmd\fR will start new plugin processes on behalf of
a single client user id.

The concurrent limit above only counts plugin processes that are still
alive. A client that connects and immediately hangs up never has more than a
couple of processes outstanding, so on its own the concurrent limit does not
stop it making \fBlsmd\fR fork and exec a plugin without bound. This rate
limit bounds that churn; a client over the rate has its connection closed
immediately, without a plugin process being started.

A burst of four seconds' worth is allowed to start at once, and never less
than \fBmax-connections-per-uid\fR, so a client permitted that many
concurrent connections can still open all of them.

The default is \fB8\fR new processes per second. Set it to \fB0\fR for no
limit. A negative value is ignored and the default is used. The root user is
not rate limited.

.TP
\fBplugin-registration-timeout = 30;\fR

Seconds a client has to complete \fBplugin_register\fR (the authenticating
first call, carrying the URI and password) before the plugin process gives
up on it and exits. Once registration succeeds this deadline no longer
applies, so slow post-registration operations are never interrupted.

\fBlsmd\fR forks a plugin process per connection and hands it the client
socket before the client has proven itself in any way, so without a bound a
connection that never completes registration would wedge that worker
indefinitely.

The deadline is only enforced while the plugin is waiting on the client, so
a \fBplugin_register\fR that is already running when it passes is allowed to
finish, and is answered if it succeeds. One that fails after the deadline
ends the connection: the client gets the error but no second attempt on it.

The default is \fB30\fR seconds, generous for a normal login exchange.
Raise it if clients are slow to send \fBplugin_register\fR, or if a
plugin's array is slow enough to reject a login that clients need to be able
to retry on the same connection. The largest accepted
value is \fB3600\fR seconds. Unlike \fBmax-connections-per-uid\fR, \fB0\fR
does not disable this timeout, and neither does a very large value: anything
outside \fB1\fR to \fB3600\fR is ignored and the default is used instead,
since disabling it would reopen the connect-and-stall exposure this option
exists to close.

.SH Plugin OPTIONS
.TP
\fBrequire-root-privilege = true;\fR

Indicates plugin requires root privilege.
Without this line or set as \fBfalse\fR, the plugin will never be invoked as root
user by \fBlsmd\fR.

Please check \fBlsmd.conf\fR option \fBallow-plugin-root-privilege\fR for
detail.

.SH SEE ALSO
\fIlsmd (1)\fR

.SH BUGS
Please report bugs to
<libstoragemgmt-devel@lists.fedorahosted.org>
.SH AUTHOR
Gris Ge <fge@redhat.com>
